{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 897 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c532602a335d59cb47065ffc1b6bb86b4b3054c6e23817718f542ac6b5e82828" :edb/db-before-t 896 :edb/db-after-t 897 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534209 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:46:36.233Z" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161764 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $3x+8y=5$?\n\n1. $(7,-2)$\n2. $(22,-8)$\n3. $(15,-5)$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161764 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $3x+8y=5?$\n\n- $(7,-2)$\n\n- $(22,-8)$\n\n- $(15,-5)$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161764 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(7,-2)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=7$ and $y=-2$, we obtain\n$3x + 8y|= 5; 3 \\cdot 7 + 8 \\cdot (-2)|= 5; 21 - 16|= 5; 5|= 5✓$\n- $(22,-8)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=22$ and $y=-8$, we obtain\n$3x + 8y|= 5; 3 \\cdot 22 + 8 \\cdot (-8)|= 5; 66 - 64|= 5; 2 \\mid \\ne 5\\cdot$\n- $(15,-5)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=15$ and $y=-5$, we obtain\n$3x + 8y|= 5; 3 \\cdot 15 + 8 \\cdot (-5)|= 5; 45 - 40|= 5; 5|= 5✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161764 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(7,-2)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=7$ and $y=-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =5 \\\\ 3\\cdot 7+8\\cdot (-2) & =5 \\\\ 21-16 & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(22,-8)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=22$ and $y=-8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =5 \\\\ 3\\cdot 22+8\\cdot (-8) & =5 \\\\ 66-64 & =5 \\\\ 2 & \\ne 5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(15,-5)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=15$ and $y=-5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =5 \\\\ 3\\cdot 15+8\\cdot (-5) & =5 \\\\ 45-40 & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161765 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\n- $18x+15y=24$\n\n- $7x-28y=10$\n\n- $9x+27y=16$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161765 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\n1. $18x+15y=24$\n2. $7x-28y=10$\n3. $9x+27y=16$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161765 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: \\mid \\: c$, where\n\n$$\nd = \\text{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=18$, $b=15$, and $c=24$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(18,15)=3$\nand $3 \\: \\mid \\: 24$, our equation has solutions.\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=7$, $b=-28$, and $c=10$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(7,-28)=7$\nand $7 \\!\\not{\\mid}\\: 10$, our equation has no solution.\n- Equation III has no solution. We have $a=9$, $b=27$, and $c=16$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(9,27)=9$\nand $9 \\!\\not{\\mid}\\: 16$, our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161765 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: | \\: c,$ where $d = \\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=18,$ $b=15,$ and $c=24.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(18,15)=3\n$$\n\nand $3 \\: | \\: 24,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=7,$ $b=-28,$ and $c=10.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(7,-28)=7\n$$\n\nand $7 \\!\\not{|}\\: 10,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation III has no solution. We have $a=9,$ $b=27,$ and $c=16.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(9,27)=9\n$$\n\nand $9 \\!\\not{|}\\: 16,$ our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161766 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $2x+7y=10$. The integer pair $(\\overset{\\sim}{x},\\overset{\\sim}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x$. What is the value of $\\overset{\\sim}{x}$?" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161766 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $2x+7y=10.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{\\sim}{x},\\overset{\\sim}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x.$ What is the value of $\\overset{\\sim}{x}?$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161766 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=2$, $b=7$, and $c=10$. We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(2,7)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 3 & +\\end{vmatrix}1 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 2 &= \\end{vmatrix}1 \\cdot 2 \\mid + \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 2 \\cdot 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(a,b) = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot (-3) + 7 = 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot (-3) + 7 &= 1 \\\\\n2 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot 10 + 7 \\cdot 10 &= 10 \\\\\n2 \\cdot (-30) + 7 \\cdot 10 &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(-30,10)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left(-30+\\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\, 10-\\dfrac{a}{d}t\\right) = (-30+7t, \\, 10-2t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\,\n$$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of\n\n$$\nx=-30+7t\n$$\n\nis attained when $t=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\sim}{x} = -30+7 \\cdot 5 = 5\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161766 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=10.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(2,7)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2 & = & 1\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-2\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b) = 1$ and\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot (-3) + 7 = 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-3)+7 & =1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 10+7\\cdot 10 & =10 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-30)+7\\cdot 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(-30,10)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left(-30+\\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\, 10-\\dfrac{a}{d}t\\right) = (-30+7t, \\, 10-2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=-30+7t$ is attained when $t=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\sim}{x} = -30+7 \\cdot 5 = 5.\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161767 1071 "Given that $(x,y)=(15,14)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $11x-13y=-17$, and that $(\\overset{\\sim}{x},\\overset{\\sim}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x$, what is the value of $\\overset{\\sim}{x}$?" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161767 1071 "Given that $(x,y)=(15,14)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $11x-13y=-17,$ and that $(\\overset{\\sim}{x},\\overset{\\sim}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x,$ what is the value of $\\overset{\\sim}{x}?$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161767 1074 "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: \\mid \\: c$, where\n\n$$\nd = \\text{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $(x_0,y_0)$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(x_0+\\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\, y_0-\\dfrac{a}{d}t\\right), \\qquad t=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=11$, $b=-13$, $c=-17$, and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(11,-13)=1\n$$\n\nSince $(15,14)$ is a solution of\n\n$$\n11x-13y=-17\n$$\n\nthe general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\left(15+\\dfrac{(-13)}{1}t, \\, 14-\\dfrac{11}{1}t\\right) = (15-13t, \\, 14-11t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\,\n$$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=15-13t$ is attained when $t=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\sim}{x} = 15-13 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186161767 1074 "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: | \\: c,$ where $d = \\text{gcd}(a,b).$  Moreover, if $(x_0,y_0)$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(x_0+\\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\, y_0-\\dfrac{a}{d}t\\right), \\qquad t=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=11,$ $b=-13,$ $c=-17,$ and \n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(11,-13)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $(15,14)$ is a solution of $11x-13y=-17,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\left(15+\\dfrac{(-13)}{1}t, \\, 14-\\dfrac{11}{1}t\\right) = (15-13t, \\, 14-11t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\, .$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=15-13t$ is attained when $t=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\sim}{x} = 15-13 \\cdot 1 = 2.\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414080 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414082 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161973 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414084 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161973 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414086 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161974 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414081 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161975 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414083 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186161975 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414085 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186162209 1050 960.0 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186162610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160952 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186162610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414072 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186162610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186162610 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $6x + 8y = 10$. The integer pair $(x^(∼), y^(∼))$ is the solution with the greatest possible negative value of $x$. What is the value of $x^(∼)$?" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186162610 1071 "Consider the Diophantine equation $6x+8y=10.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the greatest possible negative value of $x.$ What is the value of $\\overset{∼}{x}?$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186162610 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=6,$ $b=8,$ and $c=10.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(6,8)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 6 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n2=8-6\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=2$ and\n\n$$\n6\\cdot (-1)+8=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (-1)+8 & =2 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5+8\\cdot 5 & =10 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-5)+8\\cdot 5 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(-5,5)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(-5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,5-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(-5+4t,\\,5-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe greatest possible negative value of $x=-5+4t$ is attained when $t=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=-5+4\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186163216 1064 "A **linear Diophantine equation** with two variables $x$ and $y$ takes the form\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are integer constants. A **solution of a Diophantine equation** is any integer pair $(x,y)$ that satisfies the equation.\n\nFor example, the Diophantine equation\n\n$$\n2x+3y=9\n$$\n\nhas an infinite number of integer solutions:\n\n$$\n(3, 1), \\quad (6, -1), \\quad (15, -7), \\quad \\ldots\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186163216 1064 "A **linear Diophantine equation** with two variables $x$ and $y$ takes the form \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are integer constants. A **solution of a Diophantine equation** is any integer pair \n\n$$\n(x,y)\n$$\n\n that satisfies the equation.\n\nFor example, the Diophantine equation \n\n$$\n2x+3y=9\n$$\n\nhas an infinite number of integer solutions:\n\n$$\n(3, 1), \\quad (6, -1), \\quad (15, -7), \\quad \\ldots\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186163217 1064 "The linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: \\mid \\: c$, where\n\n$$\nd = \\text{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nLet's use the theorem to test some Diophantine equations.\n\n- Consider the equation\n$2x+10y=17$.\nHere, we have $a=2$, $b=10$, and $c=17$. Notice that\n$d=\\text{gcd}(2,10)=2$\nand $2 \\!\\not{\\mid}\\: 17$. Therefore, according to the theorem, our equation has no solution.\n\nIndeed, notice that the expression on the left-hand side ($2x+10y$) is always an even number, while the right-hand side of the equation ($17$) is an odd number. As a result, there is no solution.\n\n- Consider the equation\n$4x+5y=7$.\nHere, we have $a=4$, $b=5$, and $c=7$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(4,5)=1$\nand $1 \\:{\\mid}\\: 7$, our equation has solutions.\n\nIndeed, one of the solutions for\n\n$$\n4x+5y=7\n$$\n\nis the pair $(3,-1)$." 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186163217 1064 "The linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: | \\: c,$ where $d = \\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nLet's use the theorem to test some Diophantine equations.\n\n- Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2x+10y=17.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=2,$ $b=10,$ and $c=17.$ Notice that\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(2,10)=2\n$$\n\nand $2 \\!\\not{|}\\: 17.$ Therefore, according to the theorem, our equation has no solution.\n\nIndeed, notice that the expression on the left-hand side ($2x+10y$) is always an even number, while the right-hand side of the equation ($17$) is an odd number. As a result, there is no solution. \n\n- Consider the equation\n\n$$\n4x+5y=7.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$ Since \n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(4,5)=1\n$$\n\nand $1 \\:{|}\\: 7,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nIndeed, one of the solutions for $4x+5y=7$ is the pair $(3,-1).$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186163218 1064 "If $(x_0,y_0)$ is any particular solution of the equation, then the set of all solutions consists of integer pairs\n\n$$\n\\left(x_0 + \\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\: y_0 - \\dfrac{a}{d}t \\right), \\qquad t = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\:\n$$\n\nIn particular, if $d=1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left(x_0 + bt, \\: y_0 - at \\right), \\qquad t = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\:\n$$\n\nFor example, given that\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(4,-1)\n$$\n\nis a solution of the Diophantine equation\n\n$$\n4x+5y=11\n$$\n\nfind its general solution.\n\nHere, we have $a=4$, $b=5$, and $c=11$. Since $(4,-1)$ is a solution of\n\n$$\n4x+5y=11\n$$\n\nthe general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4 + \\frac{5}{1}t,-1 - \\frac{4}{1}t) &= (4 + 5t,-1 - 4t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\,\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 false] [17592186163218 1064 "If $(x_0,y_0)$ is any particular solution of the equation, then the set of all solutions consists of integer pairs  \n\n$$\n\\left( x_0 + \\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\: y_0 - \\dfrac{a}{d}t \\right), \\qquad t = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\: .\n$$\n\nIn particular, if $d=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left( x_0 + bt, \\: y_0 - at \\right), \\qquad t = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\: .\n$$\n\nFor example, given that $(x,y)=(4,-1)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation \n\n$$\n4x+5y=11,\n$$\n\nfind its general solution. \n\nHere, we have $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=11.$ Since $(4,-1)$ is a solution of $4x+5y=11,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\frac{5}{1}t,\\,-1-\\frac{4}{1}t) & =(4+5t,\\,-1-4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\, .$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414032 1080 "II only" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414032 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414032 1083 #uuid "07abc408-ee18-502b-a90f-e521c92d5444" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414033 1080 "7" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414033 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414033 1083 #uuid "12d32afc-c2b4-50a2-814f-1fe63fd8b640" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414034 1080 "3" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414034 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414034 1083 #uuid "156ece71-12b3-545f-aed4-7b9af90fe229" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414035 1080 "(1-2t,\\,-1-3t)" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414035 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186414035 1083 #uuid "1638cd4e